#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088910 :tutorial/content "When an ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis, we must have $y = 0.$ So, to find the $x$-intercepts of an ellipse, we substitute $y = 0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$\n\nFor example, let's find the $x$-intercepts of the ellipse given by the equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-3)^2}{4} + y^2 = 1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=0$ into the equation and solving the resulting equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+{0}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =3\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =1,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $(1,0)$ and $(5,0).$ We can verify this by plotting the graph:\n\n![](images/8c/8c14d3e9c1738c41bf97a8e715839f53fd073ac0a502adf0dec594d6e27ef2a0.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "3b8c37af-7386-5b3e-9594-49f3b34d1830" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3410 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088912 :tutorial/content "When an ellipse intercepts the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis, we must have ![](images/29/29da0a4605613d6a7fd9e7bb2f4fb45cbabe8a47109971682844cb222486a20c.svg) So, to find the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercepts of an ellipse, we substitute ![](images/51/51a24269e3f466a9c7b886bcab70a365576b28534e323a55aec8b3ebe8d14571.svg) into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for ![](images/33/3337bbda5af4a3203ef0f6135b36de4f06c1915c4a3ff1bd5358326627c1eecd.svg)\n\nFor example, let's find the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercepts of the ellipse given by the equation\n\n![](images/56/5683b5e36c95c36af104a43f9925d36494600eb5800641dd6fee797a0e823cd1.svg)\n\nSubstituting ![](images/51/51a24269e3f466a9c7b886bcab70a365576b28534e323a55aec8b3ebe8d14571.svg) in the equation of the ellipse and solving the resulting equation for ![](images/f4/f4e3bd65ea5e543add33f4a03c10e6b51bba71ff45e1eee33a9ce7e01d064ac7.svg) we get\n![](images/f2/f2d9dd16a35b6be9f8f531de23c6c260904760a2ab07fca95ac5bdd2bd8da448.svg)\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis at the points ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) and ![](images/89/890e85845685b97022a3d948405b315a6dcb34195a3a3c7e057c18c22327276f.svg) We can verify this by plotting the graph:\n\n![](images/bf/bf692dc0a62f43c014cd0dd9a704a624e5216f1d53bf8458679cfeab052394e1.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "24adbc44-0145-5b14-a9b5-a586b757814a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3412 :tutorial/title "Finding the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088918 :tutorial/content "Some ellipses have no $x$-intercepts. For example, consider the ellipse given by the equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-3)^2}{4} + (y-2)^2 = 1.\n$$\n\nAs we can see in the graph below, this ellipse does not intercept the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/f1/f112a9054947dfd03d5b4d6c370fa56e651ea29779480c8cc40e592c89fd6823.png)\n\nAlgebraically, we can tell that an ellipse has no $x$-intercepts if there are no solutions when we substitute $y=0$ into the equation.\n\nFor example, substituting $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse given above, we find no solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+(y-2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+(0-2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+4 & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4} & =-3 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(x-3)^2 \\geq 0$, there is no $x$ such that $(x-3)^2 = -12.$ Therefore, the ellipse has no $x$-intercepts, meaning that it does not intercept the $x$-axis at any point." :tutorial/id #uuid "5f5dc1af-29bc-5b89-9d88-afd2a4ea52c4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3435 :tutorial/title "Ellipses with No x-Intercepts"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088919 :tutorial/content "Again, some ellipses have no $y$-intercepts. For example, consider the ellipse given by the equation\n\n$$\n(x-2)^2 + \\dfrac{(y-3)^2}{4} = 1.\n$$\n\nAs we can see in the graph below, this ellipse does not intercept the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/64/64e23ef486d53706055b78e2bcd4746d735c9fcf18c5462cf9da5718100574bf.png)\n\nAlgebraically, we can tell that an ellipse has no $y$-intercepts if there are no solutions when we substitute $x=0$ into the equation.\n\nFor example, substituting $x=0$ into the equation of the ellipse given above, we find no solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ (0-2)^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ 4+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{4} & =-3 \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(y-3)^2 \\geq 0$, there is no $y$ such that $(y-3)^2 = -12.$ Therefore, the ellipse has no $y$-intercepts, meaning that it does not intercept the $y$-axis at any point." :tutorial/id #uuid "01941859-2f9f-5be9-adf7-d10e754dd7e5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3436 :tutorial/title "Ellipses with No y-Intercepts"}]}
